$ enola locate
exit: 2
--- stdout
--- stderr
Usage: enola locate [flags] [<file:line>...] [repo_path|config_path]

Map places in source to the symbols the graph holds there, and remembered
symbols to where they are now.

A location is file:line or file:start-end, with the file as the graph names
it or any path ending in it. Each resolves to the innermost symbol holding it:
its fact id (the id in facts.jsonl) and its body_hash, the version of its code.
A language whose extractor measures no spans gets the nearest declaration
above, and says so.

By default it answers against the working tree, taking a snapshot when the one
on disk is stale. --at answers against a recorded revision, and --facts against
a stored snapshot, with no working tree needed.

Exit codes:
  0  the lookup ran
  2  the command could not run

Flags:
  -at string
    	answer against this recorded revision instead of the working tree (a snapshot id, git commit, HEAD~N, @<seq> or ref)
  -diff string
    	a unified diff file, or - for stdin: report the symbols its changed lines fall in
  -facts string
    	answer from a stored snapshot instead of the working tree: an output directory holding facts.jsonl, or the file itself
  -json
    	emit the result as JSON
  -side string
    	which side of the diff the graph is of: new (the tree with the change) or old (its parent) (default "new")
  -stamps string
    	a JSONL file of stamps ({id, body_hash, name}), or - for stdin: report each as current, rewritten, moved or gone
